"The cause is in \u0060OrderApi.GetOrderTotal\u0060 in \u0060src/OrderService/Api/OrderTotal.cs\u0060, at line 18. I\u0027ve recorded the investigation for run \u0060eval-order-total-1003-again\u0060, with area **code** and confidence **high**. I changed no code.\n\n- **What I checked:** The stack trace points at line 18, \u0060DiscountRates[order.DiscountCode]\u0060. In \u0060data/orders.csv\u0060, order 1003 (Grace Hopper) carries the code \u0060SUMMER25\u0060. The table in \u0060OrderTotal.cs\u0060 holds only \u0060WELCOME10\u0060 and \u0060VIP20\u0060, so the dictionary lookup throws \u0060KeyNotFoundException\u0060.\n- **Why it\u0027s a code issue:** The runbook says an unknown or retired code should give no discount (total = subtotal). The code has no fallback for that case, so it fails and blocks the checkout. The data is consistent with the runbook\u0027s warning that Marketing retires codes without telling engineering. The record is not wrong, so this is not a data fault.\n- **Fix direction:** Use a \u0060TryGetValue\u0060 lookup and return the subtotal when the code is missing. Orders with \u0060WELCOME10\u0060, \u0060VIP20\u0060 or no code would keep the same totals. The existing tests (\u0060GetOrderTotal_AppliesTheDiscountCode\u0060 expecting 26.10 for 1001, and the no-discount case for 1002) cover that path.\n- **Side note, not in this error:** \u0060GetDiscountLabel\u0060 in \u0060Api/DiscountLabel.cs\u0060 also assumes the code ends in a two-digit percentage. For \u0060SUMMER25\u0060 it would print \u0022SUMMER (25% off)\u0022 even though no discount is applied. Consider that when the fix is made.\n\n[Answered by triage.investigator, did: ReadWorkspaceFile: namespace OrderService;; ReadWorkspaceFile: using System.Globalization;; SearchWorkspace: demos/louis-agent/samples/order-service/tests/OrderService.Tests/OrderApiTests.\u2026; SearchWorkspace: demos/louis-agent/samples/order-service/src/OrderService/Program.cs:5: //   dot\u2026; ReadWorkspaceFile